# Web (Next.js App Router)

## Versions

Next.js 16.4 and React 19.3, on Node 24 (`web/.nvmrc`). Tailwind CSS v4, configured in `app/globals.css` (`@import "tailwindcss"; @plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";`); there is no `tailwind.config.js`.

This Next is newer than most training data. Before using an API you aren't sure of, read its page in `web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/` (see `web/AGENTS.md`, which `next dev` writes and keeps). Specifics that matter here:

- `cacheComponents` and `partialPrefetching` are on (both become the default in Next 17). Nothing is cached unless it runs inside a `"use cache"` function; give every one an explicit `cacheLife(...)` and a `cacheTag(...)`. Don't use the older `fetch` options (`cache: 'force-cache'`, `next: { revalidate, tags }`) or route segment exports (`dynamic`, `revalidate`, `fetchCache`). Docs: `docs/01-app/03-api-reference/01-directives/use-cache.md` and `docs/01-app/02-guides/migrating-to-cache-components.md`.
- Cached functions can't read `cookies()`, `headers()`, or `searchParams`. Read those outside the cache, inside a `<Suspense>` boundary, and pass plain values in as arguments.
- `params` and `searchParams` are Promises in pages and layouts. Pass the Promise into a component wrapped in `<Suspense>` and `await` it there, so the rest of the page can prerender.
- A cached function whose arguments are known at build time runs during `next build`. Keep API calls keyed by `params` or `searchParams` (and no `generateStaticParams`), so the build doesn't need a running Go API.
- Turbopack is the bundler for dev and build; Tailwind runs as a Turbopack CSS loader (`@tailwindcss/turbopack`), not through PostCSS.
- Formatting is Prettier with `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` (class order); ESLint uses `eslint-config-next` with `eslint-config-prettier` last.
- Run tools through `make web-*` from the repo root (see AGENTS.md), not bare `npm`/`npx`: the Makefile puts the right Node on `PATH`.
- Tests are Vitest (`vitest.config.mts`, jsdom by default). Vitest can't render async server components; keep pages thin and test the API client and small synchronous components.
- API types come from `lib/api/schema.d.ts`, generated by `make web-types`. Never hand-write types for API responses or edit that file; change `api/openapi.yaml` and regenerate.

- Server components by default. Add `"use client"` only for state, effects, or browser APIs, and keep those components small and at the leaves.
- Fetch data in server components through the typed API client. Don't call `fetch` against the Go API ad hoc in components.
- The API client lives in one place (e.g. `web/lib/api`), with types matching the Go JSON responses. Don't use `any`.
- Caching lives in the API client's `"use cache"` functions, not in pages or components.

```tsx
// BAD: client component just to fetch
"use client";
export default function Posts() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => { fetch("/api/v1/posts").then(/* ... */); }, []);
}

// GOOD: server component using the typed client
import { listPosts } from "@/lib/api";

export default async function Posts() {
  const { items } = await listPosts();
  return <PostList posts={items} />;
}
```

- Search input is a client component that updates the URL query (`?q=`); the results page stays a server component reading `searchParams`.
